<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_4_3">
    <title>Power Functions: Positive Integral Exponents</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Identify whether a given function is a power function <m>f(x) = kx^p</m> with a positive integer exponent, and determine its coefficient and exponent.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Use power functions with positive integer exponents to model and solve applied proportionality problems.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Describe and sketch the graph of <m>y = kx^p</m> for a positive integer exponent <m>p</m>, including its symmetry and the effect of the coefficient <m>k</m>, distinguishing between even and odd <m>p</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            In the next two sections we look at the properties and the graphs of the so-called power functions.
        </p>

        <definition>
            <title>Power Function</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    A function <m>y = f(x)</m> is called a <alert>power function</alert> if <m>f(x)</m> can be expressed in the form
                    <md>
                        f(x) = kx^p
                    </md>
                    where <m>k</m> and <m>p</m> are constants and <m>k \neq 0</m>.
                    The constant <m>k</m> is called the <em>coefficient</em> of the power function <m>f(x)</m> and <m>p</m> is called the <em>exponent</em>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            <alert>Note:</alert> When <m>y</m> depends on <m>x</m> according to the formula <m>y = kx^p</m>, we say that <m>y</m> is <alert>directly proportional</alert> (or proportional) to <m>x^p</m> with the coefficient of proportionality <m>k</m>.
            If <m>\displaystyle y = \frac{k}{x^p}</m>, we say that <m>y</m> is <alert>inversely proportional</alert> to <m>x^p</m> with the coefficient of proportionality <m>k</m>.
            So power functions express proportionality of the dependent variable to powers of the independent variable.
        </p>

        <p>
            As the title suggests, in this section we look at the case when the exponent <m>p</m> is a positive integer.
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which of the functions below are power functions?
                    For those which are, rewrite in the standard form <m>y = kx^p</m>.
                    Identify the coefficient and the exponent.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle f(x) = 2x^2 \cdot 4x^3</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle g(x) = (-(x^3))^5</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle h(x) = \frac{(x^2)^4}{7x^3}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle r(x) = 2x^3 + 7x^2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle m(x) = \frac{8x}{\pi ^2}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\displaystyle l(x) = 3 \cdot 2^x</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>f(x)</m> is a power function.
                                Using Rules of Exponents from Section 4.1, we can rewrite:
                                <md>
                                    f(x) = 2x^2 \cdot 4x^3 = (2 \cdot 4)(x^2x^3) = 8x^5.
                                </md>
                                The coefficient is <m>k = 8</m> and the exponent is <m>p = 5</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>g(x)</m> is a power function:
                                <md>
                                    g(x) = (-(x^3))^5 = (-1 \cdot (x^3) )^5 = (-1)^5(x^3)^5 = -x^{15}.
                                </md>
                                The coefficient is <m>k = -1</m> and the exponent is <m>p = 15</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>h(x)</m> is a power function as well:
                                <md>
                                    h(x) = \frac{(x^2)^4}{7x^3} = \frac{x^8}{7x^3} = \frac{1}{7}x^{8 - 3} = \frac{1}{7}x^{5}.
                                </md>
                                The coefficient is <m>k = \frac{1}{7}</m> and the exponent is <m>p = 5</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>r(x)</m> is not a power function.
                                It cannot be rewritten as <m>r(x) = kx^p</m>, since <m>r(x)</m> is a sum of two power functions with differing exponents <mdash/> namely <m>2x^3</m> and <m>7x^2</m>.
                                A function that is a sum of power functions with non-negative integer exponents is called a <em>polynomial function</em>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>m(x)</m> is a power function.
                                Remember that <m>\pi</m> is just a constant.
                                <md>
                                    m(x) = \frac{8x}{\pi ^2} = \frac{8}{\pi^2}x.
                                </md>
                                The coefficient is <m>k = \frac{8}{\pi^2}</m> and the exponent is <m>p = 1</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>l(x)</m> is not a power function.
                                Observe that in <m>l(x)</m> the base of the power expression <m>2^x</m> is constant and equal to <m>2</m>.
                                The exponent <m>x</m> is a variable.
                                In a power function, it is the other way around: the base is a variable and the exponent is constant.
                            </p>
                            <aside>
                                <p>
                                    Functions like <m>l(x)</m> where the variable <m>x</m> is in the <em>exponent</em> is the topic of discussion in Chapter 5.
                                </p>
                            </aside>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A ball dropped from the Empire State Building has traveled down the distance of <m>d(t)</m> feet after <m>t</m> seconds where
                    <md>
                        d(t) = 16t^2.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Is the function <m>d(t)</m> a power function?
                                If yes, identify the exponent and the coefficient.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The Empire State Building is <m>1250</m> ft tall.
                                How long will it take for the ball to hit the ground?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>d(t)</m> is a power function with <m>k = 16</m> and <m>p = 2</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The ball will hit the ground when it has traveled <m>1250</m> feet.
                                That is, for a positive <m>t</m> such that:
                                <md>
                                    d(t) = 16t^2 = 1250.
                                </md>
                                To solve the equation for <m>t</m> we divide both sides by <m>16</m>:
                                <md>
                                    t^2 = \frac{1250}{16}.
                                </md>
                                The two solutions to this quadratic equation are:
                                <md>
                                    t = \pm\sqrt{\frac{1250}{16}}.
                                </md>
                                Since <m>t</m> has to be positive, then the solution is
                                <md>
                                    t = \sqrt{\frac{1250}{16}}.
                                </md>
                                Hence, the ball will hit the ground after:
                                <md>
                                    t = \sqrt{\frac{1250}{16}} \approx 8.8 \hspace{3pt} \text{seconds}.
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        Note that in terms of proportionality, we can say that the distance <m>d</m> is directly proportional to <m>t^2</m> with the coefficient of proportionality <m>16</m>.
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>V(r)</m> be the volume of a sphere of radius <m>r</m> centimeters.
                    We know from elementary geometry that:
                    <md>
                        V(r) = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Is <m>V(r)</m> a power function?
                                If yes, find the coefficient and the exponent.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What radius is required for the volume to be 25 cm <m>^3</m>?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Yes, <m>V(r)</m> is a power function.
                                The coefficient <m>k</m> is <m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m> and the exponent <m>p</m> is <m>3</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We are looking for the radius <m>r</m>, in centimeters, such that
                                <md>
                                    \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3 = 25.
                                </md>
                                Dividing both sides by <m>4\pi</m> and multiplying by <m>3</m>, then
                                <md>
                                    r^3 = \frac{3}{4\pi}25.
                                </md>
                                Now we take the power <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> of both sides (in other words, the cube root) and obtain:
                                <md>
                                    (r^3)^{\frac{1}{3}} = \left( \frac{3}{4\pi}25 \right)^{\frac{1}{3}}
                                </md>
                                Since <m>(r^3)^{\frac{1}{3}} = r</m>, then
                                <md>
                                    r = \left( \frac{3}{4\pi}25 \right)^{\frac{1}{3}} \approx 1.814 \text{ cm}
                                </md>
                                The volume is <m>25 \text{ cm}^3</m> when the radius is <m>1.814</m> cm.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
        <title>Graphs of Power Functions: Positive Integral Exponents</title>

        <p>
            Graphs of power functions <m>y = kx^p</m> with exponents <m>p</m> that are positive integers are different for <m>p</m> even and for <m>p</m> odd.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Even Positive Exponents</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>p</m> is even, then <m>x^p</m> is positive for all <m>x</m> except for <m>x = 0</m> where <m>x^p = 0</m>.
            For example, if <m>p=6</m> then <m>1^6 = 1</m>, <m>(-1)^6 = 1</m>, <m>2^6 = 64</m>, <m>(-2)^6 = 64</m>, and so on.
            Hence, the graph of the power function <m>f(x) = kx^p</m> for any even <m>p</m> is symmetric about the <m>y</m>-axis as <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Here are the graphs of the functions <m>y = kx^p</m> for <m>p = 2, 4, 6</m> and <m>k = 1</m>.
            All graphs are U-shaped and reminiscent of the quadratic parabola <m>y = x^2</m>.
        </p>

        <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_3_1">
            <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image label="4_3_1_fig">
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
            \begin{axis}[grid=both,
            xmin=-3,xmax=3,
            ymin=0,ymax=6,
            legend pos=south east,
            xtick={-3,...,3},
            ytick={0,...,10},
            minor tick={},
            xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
            ]
            \addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {x^2};
            \addlegendentry{$x^2$};
            \addplot[domain=-2:2,red] {x^4};
            \addlegendentry{$x^4$};
            \addplot[domain=-2:2,orange] {x^6};
            \addlegendentry{$x^6$};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            The coefficient <m>k</m> in <m>y = kx^p</m>, stretches or shrinks the graph of <m>y = x^p</m> vertically.
            Additionally, if <m>k \lt 0</m>, the graph is reflected over the <m>x</m>-axis.
        </p>

        <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_3_2">
            <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image label="4_3_2_fig">
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
            \begin{axis}[grid=both,
            axis lines=middle,
            xmin=-3,xmax=3,
            ymin=-3,ymax=3,
            xtick={-3,...,3},
            ytick={-3,...,3},
            minor tick={},
            xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
            samples=250]
            \addplot[domain=-1.7:1.7] {x^2} node[right,pos=0.8] {$x^2$};
            \addplot[domain=-3:3,red]{0.5*x^2} node[below right,pos=0.65] {$\frac{1}{2}x^2$};
            \addplot[domain=-1.7:1.7,orange]{2*x^2} node[left,pos=0.65] {$2x^2$};
            \addplot[domain=-1.7:1.7,black]{-2*x^2} node[right,pos=0.65] {$-2x^2$};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            <alert>Odd Positive Exponents</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>p</m> is odd, then <m>x^p</m> is positive for <m>x \gt 0</m>, it is <m>0</m> for <m>x = 0</m>, and negative for <m>x \lt 0</m>.
            For example, let <m>p=3</m>.
            Then <m>1^3 = 1</m>, <m>(-1)^3 = -1</m>, <m>2^3 = 8</m>, <m>(-2)^3 = -8</m>, and so on.
            Hence, the graph of the power function <m>f(x) = kx^p</m> for any odd <m>p</m> is symmetric about the origin <m>(0, 0)</m> as <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Here are the graphs of the functions <m>y = kx^p</m> for <m>p = 3, 5</m> and <m>k = 1</m>.
            This time, the graphs are S-shaped.
        </p>

        <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_3_3">
            <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image label="4_3_3_fig">
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
            \begin{axis}[grid=both,
            xmin=-2,xmax=2,
            ymin=-6,ymax=6,
            legend pos=south east,
            xtick={-3,...,3},
            ytick={-6,...,6},
            minor tick={},
            xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
            ]
            \addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {x^3};
            \addlegendentry{$x^3$};
            \addplot[domain=-2:2,red] {x^5};
            \addlegendentry{$x^5$};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            The coefficient <m>k</m> in <m>y = kx^p</m>, stretches or shrinks the graph of <m>y = x^p</m> vertically.
            Additionally, if <m>k \lt 0</m>, the graph is reflected over the <m>x</m>-axis.
        </p>

        <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_4_3_4">
            <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image label="4_3_4_fig">
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
            \begin{axis}[grid=both,
            xmin=-2,xmax=2,
            ymin=-6,ymax=6,
            xtick={-3,...,3},
            ytick={-5,-3,...,5},
            minor tick={},
            xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
            ]
            \addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {x^3} node[above left, pos=0.6] {$x^3$};
            \addplot[domain=-2:2,red] {0.5*x^3} node[below right,pos=0.75] {$\frac{1}{2} x^3$};
            \addplot[domain=-2:2,orange] {-2.5*x^3} node[right,pos=0.55] {$-3x^3$};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            Here is a summary of how graphs of power functions look for positive integer exponents <m>p</m>, even and odd, and for coefficients <m>k</m> positive and negative.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="48% 32% 20%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="middle middle middle">
            <p>
                <m>f(x)=kx^p</m>
            </p>

            <p>
                <m>p</m> even
            </p>

            <p>
                <m>p</m> odd
            </p>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 30% 30%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="middle middle middle">
            <p>
                Positive coefficient, <m>k \gt 0</m>
            </p>

            <image width="100%" xml:id="fig_4_3_summary_even_pos">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a representative power function with even exponent and positive coefficient.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image label="4_3_summary_even_pos_fig">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                \begin{axis}[grid=none,
                axis lines=middle,
                xmin=-2,xmax=2,
                ymin=-1,ymax=2,
                xtick=\empty,
                ytick=\empty,
                samples=250]
                \addplot[domain=-2:2] {x^2};
                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>

            <image width="100%" xml:id="fig_4_3_summary_odd_pos">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a representative power function with odd exponent and positive coefficient.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image label="4_3_summary_odd_pos_fig">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                \begin{axis}[grid=none,
                axis lines=middle,
                xmin=-2,xmax=2,
                ymin=-2,ymax=2,
                xtick=\empty,
                ytick=\empty,
                minor tick={},
                samples=250]
                \addplot[domain=-2:2] {x^3};
                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 30% 30%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="middle middle middle">
            <p>
                Negative coefficient, <m>k \lt 0</m>
            </p>

            <image width="100%" xml:id="fig_4_3_summary_even_neg">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a representative power function with even exponent and negative coefficient.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image label="4_3_summary_even_neg_fig">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                \begin{axis}[grid=none,
                axis lines=middle,
                xmin=-2,xmax=2,
                ymin=-2,ymax=1,
                xtick=\empty,
                ytick=\empty,
                minor tick={},
                samples=250]
                \addplot[domain=-2:2] {-x^2};
                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>

            <image width="100%" xml:id="fig_4_3_summary_odd_neg">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a representative power function with odd exponent and negative coefficient.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image label="4_3_summary_odd_neg_fig">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
                \begin{axis}[grid=none,
                axis lines=middle,
                xmin=-2,xmax=2,
                ymin=-2,ymax=2,
                xtick=\empty,
                ytick=\empty,
                minor tick={},
                samples=250]
                \addplot[domain=-2:2] {-x^3};
                \end{axis}
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
    </subsection>

    <xi:include href="exercises/ex_4_3.ptx"/>
</section>